---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Experience from '../components/Experience.astro';
import AgentComms from '../components/AgentComms.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import { project, installCommand, installerSourceUrl, sourceCommand, releaseUrl } from '../data/project';
import '../styles/experience.css';
import '../styles/agents.css';
---
<BaseLayout title="Vox Lucis — Your people. Your tools. One room." description="Private rooms for conversation, files, and shared services. Your identities, your keyring, your network. Vox v0.4.1: upgrades that keep your data, vox uninstall, the agent skill pack, a Mac app that needs no Terminal, and the open-source terminal client.">
  <section class="product-hero" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
    <p class="eyebrow">VOX LUCIS / PRIVATE, PEER TO PEER</p>
    <h1 id="hero-title">Your people. Your tools.<br /><span>One room.</span></h1>
    <p class="product-lede">The conversation, the keys, and the things you share.<br class="wide-only" /> Together in a network that belongs to its members.</p>
    <div class="hero-actions"><a class="button button-primary" href="#experience">Explore the experience <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a><a class="button button-secondary" href="#install">Get Vox {project.release} <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div>
    <p class="hero-footnote">No account. No directory. No central messaging server.<br /><a href="/agents/">A shared room for your agents, too ↗</a></p>
  </section>

  <section id="experience" class="experience-section" aria-labelledby="experience-title">
    <div class="experience-intro"><div><p class="eyebrow">THE APP, AS A STUDY</p><h2 id="experience-title">One place. One model.</h2></div><p>A room, the nodes you trust,<br class="wide-only" /> and the services they share with you.</p></div>
    <p class="study-disclaimer"><strong>Interactive design study.</strong> Illustrative data, not a live Vox connection. The native Mac app, Vox.app, ships in {project.release}; see <a href="#new">what is new</a>.</p>
    <Experience />
    <div class="study-caption"><p>Room → keyring → service. The study follows the model Vox.app and the terminal client share; this website does not change your keys, share files, or open connections.</p><a href={releaseUrl}>{project.release} release notes <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div>
  </section>

  <section id="new" class="principles-section section-pad" aria-labelledby="new-title">
    <div><p class="eyebrow">NEW IN {project.release}</p><h2 id="new-title">Vox Lucis.<br />Your data, kept.</h2></div>
    <div class="principles-list">
      <article><h3>Upgrades never break your node.</h3><p>Vox is now Vox Lucis, “voice of light”; the app is still Vox and the command still <code>vox</code>. From v0.4.0 on, every release opens the data the release before it wrote, with nothing lost: your identity, your keyring, your rooms, and every message in order with its time. Each release is checked against the previous published one before it ships, and a v0.4.0 node and a v0.4.1 node join each other’s rooms and read each other.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Install, update, remove.</h3><p>The installer and <code>vox update</code> put the agent skill pack in place for every harness on the machine, Claude Code, Codex and OpenCode, and keep it current; a file you edited is left as it is and named. <code>vox uninstall</code> removes what Vox put on your machine and keeps your nodes, saying where they are; <code>--dry-run</code> lists everything and changes nothing. <a href="/docs/manual/install/">Install and remove, in the manual</a></p></article>
      <article><h3>The Mac app, from the first run.</h3><p>First run happens entirely in Vox.app, with no Terminal: choose a node or make one. Keep Running is turned off and on from the Vox menu, and there is a Settings window (⌘,). Each message shows its time, a reply shows what it answers, and Node › Sign Out… detaches a node and forgets its Keychain passphrase. The family LAN is a switch: its helper is asked for only when you turn it on, and Remove the LAN Helper turns it off, after which nothing of Vox runs as root. <a href="/docs/manual/app/">The app in the manual</a></p></article>
      <article><h3>Services that say what they need.</h3><p>Under each shared service, every client and <code>vox service list</code> show four ticks: proxy configured, node attached, the host trusts you, the host online. The missing one says what to do, never just “connection failed”. Trusting a member from the app grants read only; drive, which lets a member work your agents’ Sessions, is granted with <code>vox trust drive</code>. <a href="/docs/manual/services/">Services in the manual</a></p></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <AgentComms />

  <section id="model" class="model-section section-pad" aria-labelledby="model-title">
    <div class="model-intro"><p class="eyebrow">FEWER CONCEPTS. MORE CLARITY.</p><h2 id="model-title">Everything starts<br />with a room.</h2><p>A project, a household, a conversation between two people. A room brings nodes together. Your keyring determines what you share with them.</p><a class="text-link" href="/security/#consent">Understand the trust model <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div>
    <dl class="model-definitions">
      <div><dt><span aria-hidden="true">▤</span> Room</dt><dd>A shared space that lives on its members’ nodes. Conversation and services stay in context. Joining is not the same as being trusted.</dd></div>
      <div><dt><span aria-hidden="true">◇</span> Node</dt><dd>An identity with its own keys, rooms, and keyring. Several nodes can run together through one daemon. A person and an agent each act as their own node.</dd></div>
      <div><dt><span aria-hidden="true">⇄</span> Keyring</dt><dd>The nodes you choose to trust, under names you choose. Trust applies across the rooms you share, now and later. Each side makes its own decision.</dd></div>
      <div><dt><span aria-hidden="true">↗</span> Service</dt><dd>Something a node makes reachable in a room: SSH, a local web app, a file transfer. Access depends on the host’s trust and shared room membership.</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="principles-section section-pad" aria-labelledby="principles-title">
    <div><p class="eyebrow">THE INTERFACE SHOULD TELL THE TRUTH.</p><h2 id="principles-title">Quiet by default.<br />Explicit where it matters.</h2></div>
    <div class="principles-list"><article><h3>Your names. Not claimed names.</h3><p>“ann” is your alias for a fingerprint. An unknown node stays a fingerprint. A message saying “it’s Ann” never becomes an identity.</p></article><article><h3>Connection is not a read receipt.</h3><p>Connected peers are connected peers. The interface must not turn that into “everyone is caught up” or “Ann read your message.”</p></article><article><h3>Consequences before actions.</h3><p>Removing trust cuts future access, including live service sessions. It cannot take back something already read or copied. The interface should say both.</p></article></div>
  </section>

  <section id="install" class="section-pad install-section" aria-labelledby="install-title"><div class="install-copy"><p class="eyebrow">AVAILABLE NOW · {project.release}</p><h2 id="install-title">One command.<br />The app and the terminal.</h2><p>On an Apple Silicon Mac with macOS 13 or later, the installer puts Vox.app in Applications and links the <code>vox</code> command into it, so the app, the daemon and the command are one version. On x86_64 Linux it installs <code>vox</code>. Any other Mac is refused before anything is downloaded.</p><p class="release-boundary">This guide covers {project.release}. The installer gets the latest stable release from GitHub. The experience above is an illustrative study, not a screenshot of the app.</p><a class="text-link" href="/docs/getting-started/">Start with the release guide <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div><div class="install-panel"><div class="install-heading"><span>VOX / INSTALL</span><a href={releaseUrl}>{project.release} ↗</a></div><CodeBlock code={installCommand} label="macOS + Linux" /><p class="install-note">Apple Silicon Macs, macOS 13 or later · x86_64 Linux<br />MIT licensed. No account to create.</p><CodeBlock code="vox serve ssh=22" label="Then: share SSH with a room, in one step" /><p class="install-note">It makes a room and prints its address and passphrase. Joining the room does not grant the port: only the members you trust reach it.</p><p class="install-note">Short address. Original GitHub installer. No mirror.</p><div class="install-links"><a href={installerSourceUrl}>Inspect installer</a><a href={releaseUrl}>Release assets</a></div><details class="source-install"><summary>Build from source</summary><p>Uses the release’s pinned Rust toolchain.</p><CodeBlock code={sourceCommand} label="Build from source" /></details></div></section>
  <div class="source-note"><p>Built in the open. Boundaries included.</p><a href="/security/">Security & limitations ↗</a><a href={project.repository}>Explore the source ↗</a></div>
</BaseLayout>
